science magazine presents a special  issue on plant genomes which includes an  online interactive feature with  informational graphics animation and  videos explore how plant Genome Research  is contributing to our understanding of  plant biology and evolution and is  leading to tangible benefits for society  the past decade or so of plant genetic  research plant genomic research has been  really focused on casting as wide a net  as possible over the plant kingdom that  is to identify those bacteria algae  simple plants mosses etc that are key  points in the evolution of higher plants  that is angiosperms and gymnosperms the  trees and flowers that we have in our  yard this tremendous diversity that's  occurred through evolution can be traced  back and really identified through fully  sequenced genomes you can see how  pathways have evolved how pathways have  developed and where the bits and pieces  for pathways have come from during  evolution having the whole genome allows  us to know what all the genes are and  what their different classes of genes  are and how many genes there are in each  class and this can be useful from the  standpoint of eliminating certain genes  or making good guesses as to the  function of other genes another great  use is that we can compare the genomes  of one plant species to another and the  plants within a species so across a  population and this is a very very  useful for understanding how genes are  causing different traits to occur within  a population or between populations  between species  read more and interact with the online  feature at WWDC is mag org slash  plant genomes  science is published by triple-a  the science society
 

